WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
During COVID, policy makers tried to convince us&nbsp;
that distance education was a good substitute for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.320
classroom learning, like here when Gavin Newsom&nbsp;
said it would be equivalent to in-person classes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.600
So this isn't true. COVID school closures were a&nbsp;
disaster and we keep getting new evidence showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:21.480
how bad it really was. This latest study found&nbsp;
that the more time kids spent in online school the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.280
worse they did on standardized tests. The study&nbsp;
looked at State testing data for third through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:32.160
eighth graders before and after the pandemic.&nbsp;
Pass rates for students in classrooms were 13&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.920
points higher in math and seven points higher in&nbsp;
English compared to kids who were fully online.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.900
Similarly the latest scores on the National&nbsp;
Assessment for Educational Progress tests showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:48.060
the loss of two decades of progress in reading&nbsp;
and math. ACT test scores saw a similar drop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.860
dipping to their lowest point in three&nbsp;
decades in 2021. So the evidence is clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.480
Closing schools was a terrible idea that&nbsp;
left a generation of kids falling behind.

